# Accessibility

Accessibility is built into components at authoring time — never retrofitted. A surface
without correct roles, labels, and states is incomplete, not "shippable with a follow-up."

## Required attributes

| Surface | Required |
|---|---|
| Button-like | `accessibilityRole="button"`, explicit label, `accessibilityState={{ disabled, busy }}` |
| Tab | `accessibilityRole="tab"`, `accessibilityState={{ selected }}` |
| Checkbox / switch | correct role + `accessibilityState={{ checked }}` |
| Text input | visible label, composed a11y label (label + value + error), hint when needed |
| Semantic image | `accessibilityLabel`; decorative images hidden from the a11y tree |
| Header / back action | labeled navigation button with an explicit back label |

## Touch targets

- Minimum touch target of **44×44 pt** (Apple HIG) / **48×48 dp** (Material) — the
  design-system primitive owns this via `minHeight` and `hitSlop`; screens never shrink it.

## Focus and forms

- Focus order follows a logical top-to-bottom reading order.
- Multi-field forms: `returnKeyType="next"` between fields, `"done"` on the last field;
  `onSubmitEditing` triggers the same action as the primary CTA.
- Modal focus is trapped by the modal provider — no custom focus traps in product code.

## Contrast and motion

- Color combinations come from documented token pairs meeting WCAG AA contrast; new
  combinations require new tokens, not inline overrides.
- Animations respect the OS reduce-motion setting where the primitive supports it.

## Web specifics

- React Native a11y props bridge to ARIA via `react-native-web` — do not duplicate with
  web-only ARIA attributes.
- Every pressable is keyboard operable (Tab to focus, Enter/Space to activate).
- Never remove the focus outline without an equivalent token-driven focus treatment.
- Hover states are an enhancement, never the only affordance.
